% Real precision, run through `xpost -d null` by run-ps-test.sh.
%
% A real carries an IEEE 754 single-precision significand, the width
% PLRM Appendix B.1 states its real limits from, and it carries the
% same one whatever object width the interpreter was built for. The
% object width sizes the fields that reach memory; it is not part of
% the arithmetic the language performs, and a build that widened the
% significand would round differently from its sibling. What that
% costs is not a digit at the end of a printed number: a program that
% branches on a rounded comparison takes a different branch, so two
% builds of one interpreter disagree about what a program means. The
% two comparisons at the foot of this file are such branches, taken
% from encoders that choose a symbol's shape and an encoding mode.

/failcount 0 def
/assert { exch { pop } { (FAIL: ) print print (\n) print /failcount failcount 1 add store } ifelse } def

% --- the significand is twenty-four bits ---
% One past the last exactly representable integer folds back onto it,
% and the one below it does not: that pins the width from both sides,
% where testing only the fold would also pass for anything narrower.
16777216.0 1.0 add 16777216.0 eq (2^24 + 1 is not distinct from 2^24) assert
8388608.0 1.0 add 8388608.0 eq not (2^23 + 1 is distinct from 2^23) assert
16777215.0 1.0 add 16777216.0 eq (2^24 - 1 + 1 reaches 2^24) assert

% --- and the shortest decimal that reads back to a real says so ---
1.0 3 div 20 string cvs (0.33333334) eq (a third prints to nine digits) assert

% --- a running total reaches a whole number from the far side ---
% An encoder that scores a candidate in thirds and then takes the
% ceiling of the score reads three thirds as having passed the whole
% number, not as having landed on it.
1.0 3 { 2 3 div add } repeat ceiling 4.0 eq (three thirds ceil past their sum) assert

% --- and a shape ratio that ties is broken one way, not either way ---
% Comparing sqrt(a/r)*w against sqrt(a*r)*h with w/h equal to r is an
% exact tie, so the comparison reports whichever side rounding favours.
% It must report the same side in every build.
1020 1.5 div sqrt 39 mul 1020 1.5 mul sqrt 26 mul lt
(a tied shape comparison falls to one side) assert

failcount 0 eq { (SUCCESS) = } { (FAILURES: ) print failcount = } ifelse
